$DIR/main.p:12:10: (*pf) holds a view of v (the function literal, line 9), which was moved into eat (its parameter takes a L by value) at line 11, so (*pf) can no longer be used here
    (*pf), part of pf, looks at memory that v owns, without owning it: the view
    was made by the function literal at line 9. A view of an owner held in a
    local variable may be used only while the variable keeps the value the view
    was made from. At line 11 v was moved into eat (its parameter takes a L by
    value), so the view may look at memory that was released, or at a value that
    now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use (*pf) before line 11, or move v only after the last use of (*pf)
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)

